@@ -0,0 +1,141 @@
+# Opt-in measurement emission (SUP-908)
+
+Based on e2b-dev/firecracker `431f1fc7d47f4cfe1dfe42437b9394f20972b65d`.
+This protocol proves correlated emission only. It does not claim network
+quiescence, whole-lifetime coverage, collector persistence, or cloud custody.
+
+Configure `PUT /metrics` before workload admission:
+
+```json
+{"metrics_path":"/path/to/metrics.fifo","measurement_incarnation":"collector_epoch_1"}
+```
+
+Without `measurement_incarnation`, the legacy stream and FlushMetrics behavior
+are unchanged. Incarnation and request IDs are ASCII alphanumeric, dash or
+underscore tokens of 1 through 128 bytes. The collector must select a fresh
+incarnation per process and bind it to independently verified producer identity.
+
+Request a correlated emission, including preboot baseline capture:
+
+```json
+{"action_type":"FlushMeasurement","measurement":{"incarnation":"collector_epoch_1","request_sequence":1,"request_id":"baseline"}}
+```
+
+Each API request sequence starts at one and advances exactly by one. Identity
+is the full `(incarnation, request_sequence, request_id)` tuple. An exact retry
+of the last accepted request returns its original success or failure without
+serializing metrics again, even after intervening periodic writes. Only one
+last-request result is retained. Older, skipped, zero or conflicting request
+sequences fail without writing. Request IDs are not a separate global replay
+registry. Malformed or wrong-incarnation requests do not consume attempts.
+
+HTTP 200 returns the exact emitted frame header:
+
+```json
+{"schema":"sig.fc-measurement.v1","incarnation":"collector_epoch_1","attempt_sequence":2,"request_sequence":1,"request_id":"baseline","loss_detected":false}
+```
+
+Every opted-in periodic/startup/legacy-FlushMetrics write uses the same writer
+mutex and attempt sequence. Its request fields are null. Stream records are
+newline-delimited objects `{ "measurement": <header>, "metrics": <legacy payload> }`.
+Attempts start at one and are assigned before serialization. Counters and
+sequences are unsigned 64-bit values; consumers must not parse through lossy
+floating-point numbers. Measurement attempt sequence overflow rejects further
+emission without wrapping; request sequence exhaustion rejects new requests.
+Existing metric counter arithmetic is unchanged and does not acquire this checked
+overflow guarantee.
+
+The producer serializes exactly once, writes the complete frame plus newline,
+and flushes the sink before returning success. This is not fsync or a collector
+acknowledgement. Any serialization, oversized-frame, partial write, or flush
+failure sets sticky `loss_detected` for this incarnation. Legacy delta fields
+can already have advanced before failure; subsequent success never repairs
+that loss. An unsuccessful requested attempt is cached just like success.
+Frames are capped at one MiB after serialization. An incomplete prefix may
+remain after sink failure, so consumers must mark malformed/gapped streams
+incomplete rather than skip them and claim complete coverage.
+
+A replayed successful receipt describes its original emission, not a fresh
+observation of later sticky-loss state. Consumers verify contiguous evidence
+through their target receipt and retain later detected loss according to the
+measurement-window protocol. Uninitialized and mode-disabled measurement
+requests return errors, never a successful empty response. Normal legacy
+`FlushMetrics` retains its original behavior when measurement is not enabled.
+
+Verification mapping:
+
+- `measurement_periodic_interleaving_and_replay`: actual Metrics writer ordering,
+  distinct periodic and request sequences, exact cached success and conflicts.
+- `measurement_partial_write_and_flush_errors_are_sticky_and_replayed`: failed
+  requested emission, exact retry and later sticky-loss receipt.
+- `measurement_serialization_failure_poison_and_overflow`: consumed delta before
+  serialization failure, sticky poison, attempt exhaustion.
+- `measurement_requires_initialized_opt_in_and_valid_identity`: initialization,
+  mode, validation and request sequence exhaustion.
+- `test_parse_measurement_action`, `test_convert_to_response`, and
+  `test_measurement_action_preboot_requires_configured_stream`: API request,
+  response, and real preboot dispatch paths.
+
+SUP-907 still owns reader readiness, start boundary, network cutoff, joined
+persistence and protected remote receipts. Neither a successful HTTP response
+nor a changed reader pointer makes a complete measured window.
+
+
+## Irreversible runtime cutoff (SUP-909)
+
+`FinalizeMeasurement` is runtime-only and accepts the same `measurement` request
+object as `FlushMeasurement`. It requires the next fresh request sequence; a
+previous ordinary flush cannot be promoted into a terminal receipt. Malformed,
+uninitialized, legacy-mode, stale and exhausted requests fail before latching.
+
+The writer lock covers freshness validation, terminal identity reservation,
+irreversible VMM latch, vCPU pause acknowledgment and terminal serialization.
+The adapter retains control from the EventManager while paused or terminal. It
+never releases callbacks because a rejected Resume opcode was received. The VMM
+also rejects resume before kicking virtio devices. Once latched, pause failure,
+writer failure or response delivery failure cannot reopen execution; terminate
+the process after reconciliation. A failed pause acknowledgment is incomplete,
+including possible continued guest execution, even though device dispatch stays
+frozen.
+
+The successful API response is distinct:
+
+```json
+{"schema":"sig.fc-terminal-measurement.v1","cutoff":"last_completed_device_operation","measurement":{"schema":"sig.fc-measurement.v1","incarnation":"collector_epoch_1","attempt_sequence":4,"request_sequence":2,"request_id":"final_2","loss_detected":false}}
+```
+
+The corresponding stream frame retains `measurement` and `metrics`, adding
+`terminal_cutoff: "last_completed_device_operation"`. Exact terminal request
+replay returns the original success or error without pausing or serializing
+again. Other terminal identities conflict. Only terminal replay and instance,
+version and machine-configuration reads are allowed postterminal. Snapshots,
+device updates, MMDS changes, Pause, Resume and ordinary measurement flushes
+are rejected. The shared writer rejects all later emission attempts, including
+periodic and exit-related flush calls. A partial failed terminal write is not a
+successful final frame. Receipt delivery and collector durability remain separate.
+
+The cutoff covers counted operations completed before this API callback's
+pause acknowledgment; pending device callbacks, TAP traffic and guest queues are
+not drained. It makes no provider-billing, network-delivery or whole-lifetime
+claim. Measurement must be configured and a baseline established before admitted
+work. Process restart or restoring an older snapshot is a different incarnation;
+the latch is intentionally not a snapshot lifecycle protocol.
+
+Non-KVM regression cases exercise the actual writer finalization method, parser,
+HTTP response converter and adapter dispatch loop with injected state responses.
+The terminal allowlist is exercised directly. Explicit KVM/TUN tests exercise
+actual VMM paused/terminal state and rejected resume, a disconnected real
+VcpuHandle, and an attached activated net device with pending TX descriptors.
+The net test positively verifies that ordinary resume counts one frame, then
+terminal rejection preserves the pending second frame and byte count. These
+use `/dev/kvm`, `/dev/net/tun` and `CAP_NET_ADMIN`; they are ignored by default
+and must be explicitly run with the `kvm_terminal_measurement` filter and
+`--ignored`. This is complementary to the production dispatch-loop regression.
+The separately isolated `kvm_runtime_finalize_measurement_action_isolated` test
+configures global METRICS with a real temporary LineWriter and drives the actual
+runtime baseline, terminal action, replay and rejection paths, matching the
+terminal stream header to the API receipt. It must run alone with `--ignored`
+because it terminalizes the process-wide singleton. Its default VMM fixture has
+no running vCPU handles. A single integrated same-epoll-batch test and a booted guest's vCPU pause
+acknowledgment/live network cutoff remain open acceptance gates. No test-only
+VMM API is exported merely to cross the firecracker/vmm crate test boundary.
